{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/graphdrawing\/trees\/rootedtree\/reingoldtilfordlayout()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"The tidy drawing of the tree: Reingold and Tilford’s layered tree drawing as Walker","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"refined it, in Buchheim, Jünger and Leipert’s linear-time form.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"reingoldTilfordLayout","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:12GraphDrawing0B0V","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Drawing","text":"Drawing","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The root is at (0, 0) and a vertex at depth d has y = d. Children are placed left to right","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in ","type":"text"},{"code":"children(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order, vertices at one depth at least 1 apart along each subtree’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"contour, and every parent centred over its first and last child. Where two subtrees had to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"be pushed apart, the smaller subtrees between them are spaced evenly (Walker’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"improvement), so a mirrored tree draws as the reflection of the drawing and isomorphic","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subtrees are drawn identically up to translation. Coordinates are ","type":"text"},{"code":"Double","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" arithmetic:","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"separations and centring alone give multiples of a power of ½, which are exact, but the even","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"spacing divides a shift by the number of gaps it is spread over, so values such as ±5\/6","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"occur and are rounded (and a mirrored tree’s drawing then matches the reflection only to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"rounding).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Reingold and Tilford, “Tidier drawings of trees”, IEEE Transactions on Software","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Engineering SE-7(2), 1981.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Walker, “A node-positioning algorithm for general trees”, Software: Practice and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Experience 20(7), 1990."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Buchheim, Jünger and Leipert, “Improving Walker’s algorithm to run in linear time”,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Graph Drawing (GD 2002), LNCS 2528, 2002."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n) time and memory, with no recursion."}]}]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing","doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees","doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees\/RootedTree"]]},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees\/RootedTree\/reingoldTilfordLayout()"},"metadata":{"externalID":"s:5Trees10RootedTreeV12GraphDrawingE21reingoldTilfordLayoutAD0E0VyxGyF","extendedModule":"Trees","role":"symbol","title":"reingoldTilfordLayout()","conformance":{"conformancePrefix":[{"type":"text","text":"Conforms when"}